import type { StudyChapter } from "@/lib/acca-study-content"

/*
 * TX-GLOBAL · Area A — the purpose and administration of taxation. Chapters 1–3.
 *
 * ── What this track is, and what it is not ───────────────────────
 * There is no ACCA "TX-Global" exam. ACCA examines TX only as jurisdiction variants —
 * UK, Ireland, Malaysia, Singapore and the rest — and a candidate sits the one printed on
 * their exam entry. This track exists for a different reader: somebody who has not yet
 * chosen a variant, who is studying tax before their jurisdiction's paper is available to
 * them, or who wants the transferable reasoning that survives a change of country.
 *
 * The product says so in both places it is offered ("International foundation · not an
 * ACCA exam variant"), and every chapter here repeats it rather than letting a learner
 * drift into thinking this is exam preparation. That honesty is the whole point of the
 * track: it is worth building well precisely because it must never be mistaken for the
 * real thing.
 *
 * ── Why it was rebuilt ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * The previous version was SEVEN chapters generated by a .map() over a table of eight
 * term/definition pairs per syllabus area — one section each, three blocks, 2,967 words
 * for the whole track. Every chapter had the identical shape, the identical outcomes and
 * the identical two exam traps, because they were produced by a template rather than
 * written. At one chapter a day that was a seven-day course, and it taught vocabulary
 * rather than method.
 *
 * ── The rule this track is built on ──────────────────────────────
 * NO RATE, THRESHOLD, BAND OR TIME LIMIT IS EVER STATED AS FACT. Every one of them is a
 * jurisdiction's political choice and none of them transfers. What transfers is the
 * STRUCTURE: that a liability is a base multiplied by a rate, that residence and source
 * decide whose base it enters, that a deduction and a credit are worth different amounts,
 * and that computing a number is only half of an answer. Scenarios supply their own
 * figures in a neutral currency (CU) and neutral jurisdictions (Jurisdiction A, B, Z).
 *
 * There are no syllabusRefs on these chapters, because there is no official syllabus to
 * reference and inventing codes would imply one exists.
 *
 * All wording is ORIGINAL Scholify teaching text.
 */

